WEBVTT

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.860
Today, I've got a brain bender for Globie.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.770
Did you know the oceans are rising?

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.910
Have you ever wondered how scientists know this is happening?

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:25.040
Engineers and scientists working together design satellites that can measure changes on Earth,

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.820
like changes in the ocean's height.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:34.250
In fact, NASA develops satellites that can use radar height sensing.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.680
The latest one is called Jason-2.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:44.190
I talked to Josh Willis at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.740
to see how the satellite Jason-2 works.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.080
Jason-2 measures the sea level.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.120
It measures how high the oceans are.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:54.910
And how does it work?

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.970
Jason-2 basically bounces a radar wave or a radio wave.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.640
wave off the surface of the ocean and then measures the amount of time it takes for that

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.700
wave to come back. And using that information, we can tell how tall the ocean is in a certain

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.220
place or how high it is.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:19.470
Jason-2 was launched into orbit on June 20th of 2008 as part of the Ocean Surface Topography

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:26.020
Mission. The newly launched satellite measures heights of the sea surface using radar height

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.980
sensing. The Jason-2 satellite sends a pulse of

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.480
radar energy to the ocean's surface and measures how much time it takes for the

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.340
pulse to return. The longer it takes, the lower the ocean. The shorter it takes, the

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:45.600
higher the ocean. You might wonder why the work of this mission is so important.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.650
Well, if you consider that half of our world's population lives nearly 100

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:58.040
kilometers or about 62 miles off a coast, rising ocean levels are a big deal. This

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.900
satellite also measures how warm

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.430
earth is getting. Most of the earth is covered by oceans. Water stores heat better than land.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:13.500
Stored heat has a big impact on our climate. Scientists are concerned that we're increasing

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.020
earth's temperatures by generating carbon dioxide. Researchers have found that there

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:22.940
are consequences to an increase in earth's temperatures. Increasing temperatures are

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:28.440
melting ice sheets in Antarctica and Greenland. The ocean is rising three millimeters a year.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:37.950
Jason-2 and the Ocean Surface Topography Mission are vital tools giving scientists a bird's eye view of climate changes

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.340
and important clues about how the oceans and ice sheets change in a warming world.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:51.650
I don't know about you, but I find it really amazing how a satellite over 1250 kilometers above the Earth

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.340
can measure the height of an ocean within centimeters.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.970
But I guess what's even more impressive is how we can use it to help us plan for the future of our world.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:00.360
We'll be right back.
